    "cultural-notes": {
      "nodeId": "542:1015",
      "lines": [
        {
          "text": "🌍 Cultural & Usage Notes",
          "depth": 1,
          "name": "🌍 Cultural & Usage Notes"
        },
        {
          "text": "🇬🇧 vs 🇺🇸 Vocabulary",
          "depth": 2,
          "name": "🇬🇧 vs 🇺🇸 Vocabulary"
        },
        {
          "text": "British: \"tap\" / American: \"faucet\". British: \"bath\" (verb) / American: \"take a bath\". British: \"flannel\" / American: \"washcloth\". British: \"loo\" or \"toilet\" / American: \"bathroom\" or \"restroom\".",
          "depth": 2,
          "name": "British: \"tap\" / American: \"faucet\". British: \"bath\" (verb) / American: \"take a bath\". British: \"flannel\" / American: \"washcloth\". British: \"loo\" or \"toilet\" / American: \"bathroom\" or \"restroom\"."
        },
        {
          "text": "Bathroom vs Restroom vs Toilet",
          "depth": 2,
          "name": "Bathroom vs Restroom vs Toilet"
        },
        {
          "text": "In American English, 'bathroom' refers to the room at home. In public, people say 'restroom'. In British English, 'toilet' is the standard word. Asking for the 'toilet' in the US can sound too direct.",
          "depth": 2,
          "name": "In American English, 'bathroom' refers to the room at home. In public, people say 'restroom'. In British English, 'toilet' is the standard word. Asking for the 'toilet' in the US can sound too direct."
        },
        {
          "text": "Compound Words",
          "depth": 2,
          "name": "Compound Words"
        },
        {
          "text": "Many bathroom items are compound words: toothbrush, toothpaste, hairbrush, washcloth, bathtub, shower head. Learning them as units (not word-by-word) is essential.",
          "depth": 2,
          "name": "Many bathroom items are compound words: toothbrush, toothpaste, hairbrush, washcloth, bathtub, shower head. Learning them as units (not word-by-word) is essential."
        },
        {
          "text": "Morning Routine Verbs",
          "depth": 2,
          "name": "Morning Routine Verbs"
        },
        {
          "text": "English has specific verb + noun pairs: brush teeth (not wash), comb hair (not brush hair, unless using a hairbrush), take a shower (not make a shower), turn on the faucet (not open).",
          "depth": 2,
          "name": "English has specific verb + noun pairs: brush teeth (not wash), comb hair (not brush hair, unless using a hairbrush), take a shower (not make a shower), turn on the faucet (not open)."
        },
        {
          "text": "Formal vs Informal",
          "depth": 2,
          "name": "Formal vs Informal"
        },
        {
          "text": "\"Wash up\" (informal) = wash your hands and face. \"Freshen up\" (polite) = go to the bathroom to tidy yourself. \"Use the facilities\" (very formal) = go to the toilet.",
          "depth": 2,
          "name": "\"Wash up\" (informal) = wash your hands and face. \"Freshen up\" (polite) = go to the bathroom to tidy yourself. \"Use the facilities\" (very formal) = go to the toilet."
        }
      ]
    },
